---

The parts of a real page want different things. A menu needs JavaScript, a greeting that names the visitor has to be rendered for each request, a long block of prose inside an interactive widget should never reach the bundle, and a search result may be a component the server picks at runtime. ogygia handles all of these with one primitive, the **region**: a part of the page with its own rules for where its HTML comes from and when it wakes up.

Every marked import compiles to the same `<Region>` envelope, whether it ends up as an island, a server island, a lake or a held region. Because there is only one envelope, there is one way to hydrate, one way to sign a request and one way to pass props, so once you understand how a region behaves you understand all four kinds, and the other pages in this section describe settings rather than separate systems.

## The two dials

You configure a region with two independent dials, set as import attributes:

| Dial     | Question it answers                             | Values                                                                                                                        |
| -------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `render` | Where does the HTML come from, and when?        | inline in the SSR pass of the page (default) · `'deferred'`: fetched from a signed endpoint on each request                   |
| `wake`   | Does this ship client JS, and when does it run? | none (default, HTML only) · `'load'` · `'idle'` · `'visible'` · `'interaction'` · a media query · `'none'` (frozen by intent) |

A deferred region takes its timing from a `fetch` key with the same words instead of from `wake`, because it has no JavaScript of its own; [Server islands](/docs/regions/server-islands) explains why. Each named kind below is one point in this grid.

## The kinds

| Kind                                          | Mark                          | What it is                                                                                                                                                        |
| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| [Client island](/docs/regions/client-islands) | `with { wake: 'visible' }`    | Renders inline during SSR and hydrates on a schedule. This is the interactive kind.                                                                               |
| [Server island](/docs/regions/server-islands) | `with { render: 'deferred' }` | HTML rendered from a signed endpoint on each fetch, so it is fresh on every request. It streams into a static shell, and islands inside it wake after it arrives. |
| [Lake](/docs/regions/lakes)                   | `with { wake: 'none' }`       | Frozen server HTML that ships no JS and stays unchanged through SPA swaps.                                                                                        |
| [Held region](/docs/regions/held-regions)     | `region(Comp, props)`         | A region as a **value**: made in a load or a remote function, sent over the wire, and placed with `<Region of={value} />`.                                        |

Markup you do not mark is not a region at all. It is plain server HTML compiled into the page, which is the default and the cheapest thing on the site.

## What each region guarantees

The settings vary, but the envelope makes the same promises for every kind:

- **SSR first.** A region renders real HTML on the server, and any JavaScript attaches to that HTML instead of replacing it, so the markup stays when JavaScript is off.
- **One hydration.** A region hydrates once and adopts its server DOM in place, with no discard-and-recreate and no flash. Nested regions join their parent's single hydration.
- **Signed capabilities.** Every later fetch of a region (a deferred hole, an SWR remount) goes through an endpoint guarded by an HMAC capability made at SSR time, so the client holds a ticket rather than an open door.
- **Props cross by value.** Captured host values cross the boundary through devalue: plain data, `Map`, `Set`, `Date` and your registered [transportables](/docs/data-state/state-context). Functions and promises do not cross, so state that must stay live has to live inside the region.

## Shared mechanics

Two pages apply to every kind. [Timing & nesting](/docs/regions/timing-nesting) is the full reference for the schedule words, `visible` margins and regions inside regions. [Client islands](/docs/regions/client-islands) explains the marked import itself, which is a real component binding you can pass around, render dynamically or use in an `{#each}`. Most pages start with a client island, so that page is the natural next read.

